# Line endings, pinned per file type rather than left to each machine's
# core.autocrlf setting.
#
# setup.sh MUST check out with LF. A shell script with CRLF endings fails on
# macOS with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" - the \r becomes part of the
# interpreter path. It is one of the least obvious errors a new user can hit,
# and it would happen to every Mac classmate cloning from a Windows-authored
# repo without this line.
*.sh text eol=lf

# setup.cmd is the mirror case: cmd.exe wants CRLF, and a .cmd file with bare
# LF endings can mis-parse multi-line blocks.
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell reads either, but keep them consistent with the platform that
# edits them most.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Markdown and JSON are read by tools on both platforms; normalise to LF in the
# repository and let git hand each checkout what it wants.
*.md text
*.json text
